Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/geomorph-r-package/c/lY8R8skYVRk/m/EI2k5eXcAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Russell,

as far as technicality is concerned, there is no issue with having two landmarks coincide in their coordinates (not in theory and certainly not in geomorph, no idea where that info came from!).

>From the biological point of view, I don´t see any issue with what you are proposing either, insofar you maintain operational homology of landmarks and you make sure that digitizing the two cusps as one point in some taxa accurately represents shape variation in your system. I don´t know much about teeth variation to that detail, so I cannot picture if by "becoming connate" you mean that they fuse or that these are two points that can be further apart or can "come together" in some taxa. If the second, I see no problem whatsoever. Beware, however, that if one structure is lost or becomes fused in a way that makes the points of reference (initial cusps) inexistent, you might be violating the assumption of operational homology when placing landmarks.

Hope this helps.

Antigoni

